A||||36||fierosound||Stupid Mechanics - what damage did they do?||||1||
Z||000000||fierosound||02-12-2000||01:03 PM||||I think we all have stories of incompetent mechanics, and not just with our Fieros.  I've gotten fairly lucky and don't have a real Fiero "horror story" to tell, but I did have this happen.<P>I had to take my Fiero in to get the water pump replaced.  When I got it back, everything was fine.  Until...  I went to gas up.  The gas door release lever was broken off.  I suppose someone mistook it for the trunk release, and thought they weren't yanking on it hard enough, 'till they broke it off!<P>I had to pull the sail panel to pop the door to gas up and replace the lever.  Of course the dealer didn't have one in stock.  Luckily I was able to get one from a wrecker and fix it the same day!<p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 02-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||HF6582||02-12-2000||04:00 PM||||I took off the valve cover today to replace the gasket and what do i find no gasket just a bunch of sealant. And I wondered why it leaked. I am so glad this world is full of such smart people. Sorry just venting.<P>Scott||||reg||13
Z||000002||Rodrv6||02-12-2000||07:10 PM||||Scott,<BR>Before you get too mad, the four cylinder engines came from the factory without valve cover gaskets.  They use black RTV instead.  I've never taken a 6 cylinder apart, so I don't know if they are the same way.||||reg||1
Z||000003||overlord||02-12-2000||07:22 PM||||when i first bought my fiero...i thought i needed the plugs to be changed...it was running on 3 cylinders so i took it into canadian tire to get them changed.<P>when it came out..the guy said "you must of got dirt in the engine" cause now it was running on 2 cylinders.<P>so as mad as i was, i drove an hour home (and a full tank of gas) and took it to our local mechanic.<P>turns out the canadian tire dude didnt put 2 of the wires back on the plugs..(stupid retard) (dirt in the engine eh?)<P>thats the last time i take my car there.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierosound||02-12-2000||08:01 PM||||Overlord's story just reminded me of another instance not long after I bought the car (1991).  I had a cast on my arm so I couldn't do the work myself.  My car was running odd.  Whe it was cold, it was fine.  Once it warmed up, it would start missing and lurching.  I took it to a Pontiac Dealer to have them check it out.<P>They thought it was the tach filter, they didn't have once in stock, but they'd have one the next day (from the warehouse).  I had to leave my car overnight.  They next day, they install the tack filter - no difference.  Now they think , it may be the module, but guess what, not in stock.  They have it the next day, had to leave my car again.  They put that on - the problems is still there!! <P>Finally, someone suggests trying a set of new plug wires - and guess what - IT'S FIXED.  When I see the wires, two had been burnt completely through by the exhaust manifold.<P>Question...  Wouldn't all that electronic equipment and even the common sense a mechanic should have indicated right away a problem with spark plug firing???  I mean, these are trained technicians GUESSING at what the problem may be???<P>I hate having to pay GOOD MONEY to someone  to do something, then find out they know less (it seems) than I do!!!  ARRGHHHH!!!<P>||||reg||10
Z||000005||HF6582||02-12-2000||08:10 PM||||This was the orange goo that was put on so thick that I bet it took them a hole tube to do it. It took me about two hours to get all that stuff off.<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000006||wkayl||02-12-2000||09:12 PM||||Section 5 page 4 of 1986 Fiero owners manual: "Should the remote fuel door handle fail to operate, the fuel fill door can be opened using a small rectangular piece of flexible plastic (like a credit card). Carefully insert the card between the fuel fill door and the body at the center of the door until the fuel door opens. Moving the card up and down while pushing it in will assist you oin this operation."||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phaeton||02-12-2000||10:14 PM||||I had my frt upr a-arm replaced and a four wheel alignment done.  When I got the Fiero home the front wobbled in the driveway, I got out and pushed the top of the wheel 2 inches!!  The mechanic said he would have tightened it better but the limited room made it too hard to hold the spindle in place and wrench the nut at the same time!  When I noticed the severe wear beginning on the inside rear tires I did not even bother to complain to them, but instead give my 2 cents on who not to use every time the subject comes up.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Sage||02-12-2000||11:19 PM||||This one really torqued my nut!  My oldest son took my youngest sons'88cp in to have the tires balanced, as the youngest one was away in school and scheduled to pick the car up the next weekend.  He left the car and walked to grab a bite to eat.  When he came back, they had the car 5' in the air and were working under it with a torch!  He told me the guy turned to him and said,"I bet you're wondering why I'm using a torch to balance your tires huh?"  Turns out they didn't know the proper lift points and pinched a hole in the passenger side coolant tube. They brazed it up pretty good,(at least it has never leaked) but jeeeeeez!  No way of knowing whether they would have owned up to it or not had they not been caught in the act, but we have a new coolant tube now. (no cost to us) Point: STAY WITH YOUR CAR! (or do it yourself!) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||13
Z||000009||william_thorniii||02-13-2000||03:13 AM||||Sound... I think that I can top that!<P>I bought my coupe from the original owner with 45K on the clock.  I did a minor tune-up but nothing major.  When the temperature got cold the car became undriveable.  The chuggle and sputtering that the engine would undergo untill it had warmed to operating temperature often stalled the engine.  I concluded that I had to take it to the dealership... I HATE electrical problems.  I thought that I would be smart and sign form that required them to call me before anything was replaced.  The next day I got a call approving a couple of hundred in misc. sensors.  I approved a couple of them and then replaced the others on my own.  That didn't work... In fact the car drove worse after that.  I took it back.  They called me in another couple of days saying that my car was done.  I was amazed but happy.  When I went to pick it up, I was astounded by the $800 charge when they had not called for my approval on any of their work.  I ended up complaining to the manager and got it half price which was still much too expensive, but I did get a new ECM out of it.  Oh, I almost forgot to mention that the problem never was fixed.  The solution.... let the car warm up in the winter.  All that it costs me is a less than a penny a day.<P>My friends... THAT is INCOMPETENCE.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000010||fieroshop||02-13-2000||10:46 AM||||As a shop I see all these things all the time. Yesterday I got a car in with the rear calypers off the car. The people who owned the Fiero hired a mechanic to put brakes on and when the mechanic got to the rear he could not teke them off nor could he finish the job and I had to tow the car to my shop and redo all the brakes. <BR>I've got a lot of horror stories about incompetent mechanics.||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||02-13-2000||12:25 PM||||National Tire and Battery, a division of Sears, is also a dangerous place to let work on your car. Any car. They seem to hire almost anyone off the street for tires. They are some of the most unprofessional service people I've ever seen. It seemed to be regular work rules to throw tires and tools around the shop.<P>At least they let you in the bay so you can keep them from putting a jack thru the floor. you have to watch them like a hawk. or they are likely to wreck the car. In additon to not knowing how to raise the car, they seem to have trouble lowering it. Mine was nearly dropped.<P>You have to watch out for all the quick lube places as well. it's rather common for cars to end up in the hole, or in some bays that are built for one way trafic to get a tire slashed on the pit curb. They also have a nasty habbit of leaving plugs loose or out.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 02-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Raydar||02-13-2000||03:59 PM||||What Ogre said...<BR>I used to take my cars to "National Assault and Battery", only because they sold the brand of tires (BFG) that I wanted. Never again. Couldn't get an alignment right, after two attempts. Couldn't get repair work done correctly. (Not just on a Fiero. This was on an S-10. There are MILLIONS of those, so I don't think they had an excuse...) Instead of going back, and further elevating my blood pressure, I just chose to tell my friends. So there ya' go.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||02-13-2000||06:46 PM||||I have used NTB and had no problems. I never take the car in though. I take off my wheels, and stand there while they mount new tires/wheel weights. Works fine for now, but I will eventually need to find a shop competant enough to do a 4-wheel alignment.||||reg||1
Z||000014||BOILERMAKER||02-14-2000||08:33 AM||||I took my 87 SE in to get a 4-wheel alignment and when I got it back my cradle was bent and they had torched one of my tie-rods up to the point of melting claiming that it was so rusted up they couldn't align the machine properly.<P>New cradle and tie-rod proved to be quite a task.  Thank goodness for extra parts.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Deer_Slayer||02-14-2000||08:42 AM||||Last week, my stepdad and I took the 86 SE to a mechanic to have the wheels alined and have the front ball joints replaced.  When we got it back, it still vibrated and pulled to the left.  When we took it back, the mechanis said later,"We got the pull out of it, but there might be a problem with the power steering."  I don't know where he came fron because that car has NO POWER STEERING.||||reg||13
Z||000016||Monkeyman||02-14-2000||11:30 AM||||I took my car into a usually reliable tire shop recently.  The moron who tried to change my tires started to put the floor jack under the middle of the car (side).  I asked him to be careful of the water pipes (don't know the correct name).  He proceeded to explain to me that there would be no reason to have these because the motor and radiator are both up front.  ("I've had some of these Firenzas [Cav clone] before so I know all about them.")  I gently reminded the fool that this was a "FIERO" and the motor was in the back.  He said that I must have been mistaken because Pontiac never made a rear engine car.  (Technically true as it's a mid-engine.)  After I popped the trunk he proceeded to tell me that it must be modified since the motor was in the back.  You'd think, after 370,000 Fieros on the road, a few more folks would have heard of them.||||reg||1
Z||000017||DRH||02-14-2000||12:16 PM||||Monkey, I would be very careful about using that place. If he wouldn't admit he was wrong with the evidence staring him in the face... What are the chances he would own up to making <B>any</B> mistake???||||reg||1
Z||000018||Phil||02-14-2000||12:17 PM||||For my own information.Is the Fiero really a mid-engine? I thought mid engine meant that the motor was fwd of the rear wheels and rear engine the motor is behind the wheels . The Fiero is neither. ||||reg||1
Z||000019||william_thorniii||02-14-2000||12:41 PM||||Phil,<P>I see your confusion.  The way that it is considered frt/mid/rear is where the center of gravity (or mass) of the engine is located with respect to the driving wheels.  Although all of our engine is not in front of our driving wheels, the center of its mass is.  <P>Hope this helps,<P>William T. Thorn, III ||||reg||3
Z||000020||DRH||02-14-2000||12:59 PM||||I always thought it was called mid-engine because it still has a trunk.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> WT3's explanation makes more sense though.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Oreif||02-14-2000||01:02 PM||||The Fiero is mid engined. The engine is forward of the rear wheel axles. It is referenced to the axles not the physical tires. Although certain oil change places around here still insist the since there is no bolt on the oil pan it doesn't require oil. That's when she told him that the oil doesn't go in the front trunk that is where the spare tire is. It goes in the engine which is located in the rear. A friends daughter had to tell this to the manager. The mechanic had the car up on the hoist but was looking at the wrong end. ||||reg||1
Z||000022||Phil||02-14-2000||01:50 PM||||So Fieros have a radius of gyration||||reg||1
Z||000023||olympic||02-14-2000||03:48 PM||||   I believe even some front engine cars are technically "mid engined" because the engine is placed so far back in the frame.  The new Corvette and Viper are examples.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Monkeyman||02-14-2000||04:06 PM||||I think any car with the motor in front of the cockpit would be considered front engined.  The Fiero is the only mid engined, American made car.  If you guys look at a Porsche 911 you can easily see the difference between mid engine and rear engine.||||reg||1
Z||000025||william_thorniii||02-14-2000||05:02 PM||||Friends,<P>If anyone is not interested in the definition of "center of mass", please skip this post.  I am trying to not get too technical here... but this is one of my specialties.  Please do not get offended.  I am not intending this in a condescending manner, but since anyone can read this Forum want it to be clear to even a child.  Also if anyone sees anything that I miss or am wrong on, PLEASE feel free to correct me.  I would hate to pass on any incorrect information.   <P>Phil,<BR>Yes, a Fiero has a radius of gyration.  In fact every object has a radius of gyration.  However, in my post by referring to the center of gravity this was not the intended meaning.  If you have ever balanced an object by one point you have done so by placing a support in between the objects center of mass and the ground.  <P>For example, if you were to support a 2x4x8 by two fixed points located near each end the board.  The board would deflect slightly in the middle, right?  This deflection is caused by the effect of gravity on the boards mass.  Now if we wanted to discover the amount of force that each support must exert to suspend the board we would need to first create a free body diagram.  This is a picture in which all forces can be more easily represented by loads acting on a given point.  The point at which the load of the boards weight would act at the center of mass or center of gravity.  Common sense tells us that the center of gravity falls precisely in the center of the board (assuming constant density throughout the board).  So we can essentially say that the boards mass is at the midpoint of the board.  <P>The same could be done for you engine.  There is one theorhetical point that could be supported and the engine could balance on a cone.  This point is the center of mass and it is in front of the drive axles on our cars.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000026||Tom88gt||02-14-2000||05:08 PM||||1.  Buy a Haynes manual and fix it your self.<P>2.  It is a mid engine.  The rotational axis of the engine (crankshaft) is behind the driver but infront of the rotational axis of the driven wheels (axleshafts).  Therefore, in the middle.<P>3.  If you ever let someone else turn a wrench on your baby, make damn sure they know that you know what is/is probably wrong and what you expect done.<P>4.  Read no. 1 again.<P>sorry if I sound unsympathetic, but its a 12 year old car and the warranty went the way of the Dodo (extict bird).  Don't own it if you can't take care of it.||||reg||4
Z||000027||Monkeyman||02-14-2000||08:54 PM||||Tom88gt--Some of us (mostly meaning me) are fairly incompetent when it comes to mechanics.  If I only owned what I could fix, I'd be riding a Big Wheels.  The same applies to cooking (I tend to burn water).  If I only ate what I could cook, I wouldn't have "dunlap" disease.  (You know...My belly "dunlap" over my belt.)||||reg||1
Z||000028||stimpy||02-14-2000||10:17 PM||||Another point of view...<BR>Tom, I think that I am fairly competant when it comes to mechanics. However at 6'6" with a bad back, there are jobs that I would rather farm out then endure weeks of pain in the aftermath. (See Deal of the Century thread...) I definately agree that it is a darn good thing to keep my nose in the job and run from those mechanics that know less about my baby then I do. But an architect is not neccesarily able to build a house, sometimes ya gotta hire a subcontractor.<P>postscript: I like to read over in case I sound pi$$y in my posts. I totally agree about buying the manuals and knowing about your car.  I do want to add that any reputable mechanic should guarantee their work. If you have to pay more for a good mechanic that will guarantee their work, it is worth it. When I had my engine built, I asked for references, called the BBB, and made certain to check in with the shop daily. Added bonus, they hurried on the car just to get me off their butt! No complaints. <p>[This message has been edited by stimpy (edited 02-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000029||fierospeeder||02-14-2000||10:33 PM||||I wonder if anyone had the same mistakes with corvairs and the other station wagons and vans that also had the same engines.  I own a beetle and was it the same because most of the vws had the engines in the back.||||reg||1
Z||000030||Phil||02-15-2000||07:41 AM||||WTIII<BR>The radius crack was a feeble attempt at humor- my last classes in statics and dynamics were 1963 and that is about the only buzz word I remember for the "moment"||||reg||1
Z||000031||william_thorniii||02-15-2000||11:20 AM||||Phil,<P>Sorry... chalk another one up to a loss in translation from spoken word to typed word.  As every good comic knows... it's all in the delivery.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000032||Raydar||02-15-2000||04:55 PM||||I really hate to take my cars to *anybody* else to have them worked on. Only in the case of something that I *just can't fix*, or for warranty work (wife's Jeep). Under no circumstances will I EVER take anything to a dealer, otherwise. At least GA has lemon laws, so we do have SOME protection. Just my $.02. ||||reg||1
Z||000033||wkayl||02-15-2000||11:08 PM||||If you have a major problem with any consumer issue, there is a guy in Denver (Tom Martino) that has a radio show on from 12:00 to 3:00PM  Mountain time broadcast on the internet that may be able to help you.  You can e-mail him during his live show.  Listen for a while to get the jist of his show. <A HREF="http://www.troubleshooter.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.troubleshooter.com/</A>  ||||reg||1
Z||000034||JetroGT||02-16-2000||05:39 AM||||When I had my 86 GT I needed to get the engine replaced. "Something about racing a caddy with the north star at 120 for 5 minutes". I took it to to a place in town to get a reman engine. I wated almost a month! When I got it back it ran fine. Then after a week I lost almost all my oil pressure and limped back home. They claimed that a rocker had broken. After a few days I got it back again and tried to drive it to work. I got on the interstate and it ran out of gas. Not because I wasent looking but because gas was leaking out of the cold start injector at the fuel rail! Again I brought it back. This time they replaced the engine again because it had a bad knocking sound to it along with the leaking gas. Another month. This time was better. It ran good for about a month and a half untill one day I was driving down the road and orange flames started to shoot up from my back window and left air vent. At the time it was still winter so I pulled over to the side of the road, popped the trunk and proceded to take large amounts of snow which I dumped into the engine compartment. This time I did not take my car back to that place. I went to the junk yard, found a fiero with a V6 still intact. had it cut out, brought it home and stripped off what I needed. Vacume hose harnes, injector harnes, fuel rail, cold start injector. Alot of the wires I had to re-splice for the sensors. And in doing all of this work I saw that they did not put exhaust manifold gaskets on the manifolds. <BR>Ahhhh. I'm glad I finally got that out.||||reg||8
Z||000035||Tom88gt||02-16-2000||05:01 PM||||Monkey & Stimpy,<P>Points well taken.  I didn't know anything about cars when I got my first one.  But I bought a shop manual and learned as I went.  Stuff that required specific, expensive equipment (alignments) I still farm out.  But until I make $50/hr, I just can't bring myself to pay someone else to do routine maintenance and basic repairs.  Most jobs are simple (even a clutch job), some just have more steps, but each step is still basic.  <P> ||||reg||7
Z||000036||Big O||03-05-2000||04:08 AM||||Sorry to jump into thisa one so late but I just stumbled accross this in a late night cruise through the old posts.  I think my experience with bad mechanics would make you all shake your heads.  I got my 86 GT in the fall of 96.  It had 135000 miles on it and ran pretty good.  I decided that I would like to have the performance a little more interesting and so I went looking for someone to Spice up the power plant a little.  I found a shop in San Jose CA. that rebuilt engines and did "funny-cars" as a sideline.  The shop looked good, had all of the right kind of equipment and the owner was an "old world" German.  I asked him what he could do for me and he said he could bore and stroke the 2.8 out to 3.4 liters.  Fool that I was, I believed him.  I was due to go on an overseas assignment for several weeks so I let him have the car and got on the plane with dreams in my head about the neat car I would have when I got back.  When I returned from my trip I went straight from the airport to the shop to pick up my car.  Being half brain dead from 16 hours in the air, I didn't take a lot of time to closely inspect the car before I gave him his outrageous fee for fixing my car.  I drove it home and noticed that it seemed to have an exhasut leak.  I got up the next morning to find a pool of oil under the car.  BAck to the shop where the installed a new valve cover gasket and reworked the exhaust maniforl on one side where it had cracked.  Next morning, same story. Back to the shop to have them rework the other side and install another valve cover gasket.  To make a long story shorter, the car ran "well" for about a week and then started going to H@#%% in a handbasket.  It would stall, not start, overheat, and idle rough.  The mechanic couldn't seem to get it to run so I took it to another really good mechanic who I trusted who spent a great deal of his own time trying to get it to run.  They went through the entire fuel and electrical system as well as the emission control system.  Replace everything and still couldn't get the computer to baseline.  After spending an additional $2,000 on the engine I gave up and decided it just wasn't going to work.  I decided I'd sue the fool and try to get my money back.  Got a lawyer and he said I had a good case.  Just abvout them I got another overseas assignment that was going to keep me in the middle east for 3-5 years.  Lawyer says "forget the lawsuit if your not going to be here". I push the GT into the garage and forget about it for fourteen months.  BAck from Saudi I decide I've got to get the car running and so I ship it to Colorado to another shop to have the 3.8 Series II SC engine installed.  Another disaster.  After writing the Clutter family a check for $12,000, I get the car home and dump another $2,000 into fixing their meistakes.  I finally get the car running, but then have to dump another $1500 into rebuilding the transmission on the "new power plant" the Clutters have sold me.  The car now runs great and is everything I hoped for.  I think I have the only $20,000 86 Fiero GT in California and I wouldn't part for it for anything (wellll, maybe $20,000).  ||||reg||6
